<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a> includes an entirely revamped <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/genmoji/">Genmoji</a> creation experience. There's an updated interface that supports describing an emoji that you want to create, with options to start from existing emoji, choose an image from <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/photos/">Photos</a>, or select a person.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/ios-27-genmoji.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1051284" /><br/>
After uploading an image or using a text phrase to create a &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj;, there's now a "Describe a change" interface for making updates to what you've already created. You can iterate on designs and change specific elements of a &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj;, tweaking colors and objects.<br/>
<br/>
Apple's AI is smarter than before and it is able to successfully make iterative updates for better &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj; customization. Each change uses the old base rather than regenerating a new &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj; with every request. With a series of requests, it is possible to make a complicated, multi-element &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj;. Along with describing changes, you can also add in additional emoji, and the interface makes suggestions on what to do next.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj; output is also more consistent, and &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj; look more like real emoji with a 3D, cartoonish style by default. There is now an option to change style, so if you don't want the cartoonish look, you can ask for something else like a drawing or a sketch.<br/>
<br/>
Generating a &zwnj;Genmoji&zwnj; does not take as long, and it appears to be less system intensive with less battery drain.<br/>
<br/>
